{#
    One extension, as a row in a reference table.

    Not a card. A card is a self-contained object you look at one at a time, and this
    is a list you read down — someone comparing forty shipping extensions needs the
    licence column to be in the same place every time, which bordered boxes with their
    own internal layout cannot give.

    The compatibility cell carries version numbers rather than dots. An earlier
    version used ● and ◐ in fixed columns, which meant tracing every mark back to a
    header far above to learn what it referred to, across a page where two thirds of
    the cells were empty. A chip that says "6.7" needs no legend and no header.

    Wording stays "declares" and never "works with": this is the maintainer's own
    constraint from composer.json, not a test result.
#}
{# role=listitem, not role=option.

   This was a listbox, on the reasoning that the keyboard shortcuts move a selection
   rather than DOM focus. But an option may not contain interactive descendants, and
   every row holds a title link and a copy button — so the row announced as a single
   choice and the links inside it were not reliably reachable. A row is a record in a
   list, not one of forty things you pick between.

   The selection is still not focus: it stays a class, and the row's name is announced
   through the live region the shortcuts controller owns. #}
<article class="row" role="listitem" data-shortcuts-target="row">
    <div class="row-main">
        {{ include('catalog/_icon.html.twig') }}

        {# The text stacks; the mark sits beside it. Without this wrapper the flex
           row would put the title, package name and description side by side. #}
        <div class="row-text">
            <h2 class="row-title">
                <a href="{{ path('extension_detail', {slug: extension.slug}) }}">{{ extension.label }}</a>
            </h2>
            <span class="row-package">{{ extension.packageName }}</span>
            {% if extension.description and not compact %}
                <p class="row-desc">{{ extension.description|length > 160
                    ? extension.description|slice(0, 160)|trim ~ '…'
                    : extension.description }}</p>
            {% endif %}
        </div>
    </div>

    <div class="row-versions">
        {% set declared = [] %}
        {% for version in shopwareVersions %}
            {% set tier = supported[version.majorMinor] ?? null %}
            {% if tier %}
                {% set declared = declared|merge([{v: version.majorMinor, tier: tier}]) %}
            {% endif %}
        {% endfor %}

        {% if declared|length %}
            {% for item in declared %}
                {# The ring and the ˚ both mark an open-ended constraint. Two signals
                   rather than a second colour, so the distinction survives greyscale
                   and colour-vision deficiency. #}
                <span class="chip chip--version {{ item.tier == 'explicit' ? 'is-explicit' : 'is-open' }}"
                      title="{{ item.tier == 'explicit'
                          ? 'Declares support for Shopware ' ~ item.v
                          : 'Declares support for Shopware ' ~ item.v ~ ' through an open-ended constraint, which claims releases published after the extension was last touched' }}">
                    {{ item.v }}{{ item.tier == 'explicit' ? '' : '˚' }}
                </span>
            {% endfor %}
        {% else %}
            <span class="row-none" title="No parsable shopware/core constraint in composer.json">none declared</span>
        {% endif %}
    </div>

    <div class="row-licence">
        {% set licence = extension.licenseStatus %}
        <span class="chip {{ licence.value == 'unknown' or licence.value == 'rejected' ? 'chip--risk' : '' }}"
              title="{{ licence.value == 'unknown'
                  ? 'No licence file or declaration found. Public code is not the same as open source, so this is indexed and linked only.'
                  : (licence.value == 'rejected'
                      ? (extension.declaredLicenseRaw
                          ? 'Declares "' ~ extension.declaredLicenseRaw ~ '" in composer.json, which is not an accepted open-source licence. Indexed and linked only.'
                          : 'The declared licence is not an accepted open-source licence. Indexed and linked only.')
                      : 'Detected from composer.json, verified against the repository.') }}">
            {{ extension.licenseSpdx ?? licence.badgeLabel }}
        </span>
    </div>

    <div class="row-touched">
        {% if extension.lastCommitAt %}
            {# Last commit, not last release. A maintainer who has not tagged in a year
               but is still merging fixes is in a different situation from one who has
               vanished, and the release date cannot tell them apart. #}
            <time datetime="{{ extension.lastCommitAt|date('Y-m-d') }}"
                  class="num {{ extension.maintenanceStatus.value == 'abandoned' or extension.maintenanceStatus.value == 'dormant' ? 'is-stale' : '' }}"
                  title="Last commit {{ extension.lastCommitAt|date('j M Y') }} — {{ extension.maintenanceStatus.value }}">
                {{ extension.lastCommitAt|ago }}
            </time>
        {% else %}
            <span class="row-none" title="Not enriched yet, or the forge exposes no commit history we can read">—</span>
        {% endif %}
    </div>

    {# The single most frequent action here. Previously the composer line existed only
       on the detail page, and only as text to select by hand. #}
    {# For an extension Packagist does not carry, a bare `composer require` fails with
       "could not be found in any version" — which the detail page says in as many
       words while this button handed out that very command. Copying what actually
       installs it is better than copying nothing: these are the 125 extensions that
       are hardest to install, and the reason the directory exists at all.

       The repository line is deliberately visible in what gets pasted rather than
       hidden behind the button, because it adds a third-party repository to somebody's
       project and they should be able to read that before running it. #}
    {% set installCommand = extension.isOnPackagist
        ? 'composer require ' ~ extension.packageName
        : 'composer config repositories.extdir composer ' ~ url('repo_landing') ~ "\n" ~ 'composer require ' ~ extension.packageName %}

    <div class="row-copy">
        <button type="button" class="copy-btn"
                data-controller="clipboard"
                data-action="clipboard#copy"
                data-clipboard-text="{{ installCommand }}"
                aria-label="Copy the command that installs {{ extension.packageName }}"
                title="{{ extension.isOnPackagist
                    ? installCommand
                    : 'Not on Packagist — copies both lines, including the one that adds the extdir repository' }}">
            <span data-copy-label aria-hidden="true">Copy</span>
        </button>
    </div>

    {# The full badge set lives on the detail page. Repeating it here would say the
       licence and the maintenance state a second time, since both already have their
       own column.

       The disclosure badge is the exception and is not optional: where the operator
       of this directory is also the publisher of an extension, that has to be stated
       on the extension itself rather than on an about page nobody opens. #}
    {% if extension.vendor.maintainerOperated %}
        <div class="row-flags">
            <span class="chip chip--disclosure"
                  title="Published by the same person who operates extdir. Ranked by the same public algorithm as every other extension, with no special treatment.">
                extdir maintainer
            </span>
        </div>
    {% endif %}
</article>
